\title{astronomR: Cosmic Insights: Statistical Frameworks for Astronomers}
\description{
A comprehensive toolkit for astronomical and cosmological computations. Provides functions for angular coordinate conversions (degrees, hours-minutes-seconds, degrees-minutes-seconds, and radians), Julian Days and the conversion of equatorial (right ascension, declination) to horizontal (altitude, azimuth) coordinates, access to fundamental physical constants (CODATA 2018), queries to the Gaia Catalogue of Nearby Stars through the Gaia Archive TAP (Table Access Protocol) service, a Lambda-CDM cosmology model with comoving, luminosity and angular-diameter distances, cosmic time and the age of the universe, early-universe thermal physics including photon density, 'Saha' equation solutions, and a full thermal-cosmology module covering the Hubble rate in the radiation-dominated era, effective relativistic degrees of freedom, entropy density, equilibrium yields, the Boltzmann relic-abundance ('pebble') equation for WIMP freeze-out, the freeze-out temperature solver, and the Peebles equation for hydrogen recombination. Also includes the Drake equation for estimating the number of communicating extraterrestrial civilisations in the Milky Way.
}
\section{Angle conversions}{

\code{\link{deg_to_dms}}, \code{\link{dms_to_deg}},
\code{\link{deg_to_hms}} and \code{\link{hms_to_deg}} convert between
decimal degrees and degrees-minutes-seconds or hours-minutes-seconds;
\code{\link{deg_to_rad}} and \code{\link{rad_to_deg}} convert between
degrees and radians.
}

\section{Coordinates}{

\code{\link{julian_day}} gives the Julian Day of a date-time, and
\code{\link{radec_to_altaz}} converts right ascension and declination to
altitude and azimuth for an observer at a given place and time.
}

\section{Physical constants}{

\code{\link{constants_df}} is a table of physical constants and
\code{\link{constant_value}} looks a constant up by name.
}

\section{Gaia archive}{

\code{\link{get_gaia_data}} queries the Gaia archive TAP service (an
internet connection is required).
}

\section{Cosmology}{

\code{\link{cosmology_model}} builds a cosmological model that the other
functions take as their \code{cosmo} argument:
\code{\link{age_of_universe}}, \code{\link{cosmic_time}},
\code{\link{comoving_distance}}, \code{\link{luminosity_distance}},
\code{\link{angular_diameter_distance}},
\code{\link{transverse_comoving_distance}},
\code{\link{radius_of_curvature}}, \code{\link{hubble_distance}} and
\code{\link{hubble_ratio}}. Distances default to Mpc and times to Gyr.
\code{\link{redshift_to_scale_factor}} and
\code{\link{scale_factor_to_redshift}} convert between redshift and scale
factor; \code{\link{km_to_Mpc}} and \code{\link{Mpc_to_km}} convert
distances.
}

